// The fullscreen fade node, found 2026-09-09. FUN_00172810 is the game's fade
// service: an RGBA at data+0x00, a fade-in counter at +0x10 over +0x34 frames, a
// hold at +0x44, and a fade-out counter at +0x18 over +0x38 frames, with the
// blend it hands the renderer at +0x30. Every phase steps once per tick with no
// timestep, so at 60fps every fade in the game took half its real time.
// Its init, FUN_00172718, is where callers hand it durations in SECONDS and it
// multiplies by a hard-coded 30.0 - the same shape as the tween constructor. One
// word there doubles all three phases and leaves the blend curve exact, because
// the divisors at +0x34/+0x38 are copies of the same doubled counts. The 180-tick
// hold cap is a separate literal and needs doubling on its own.
[60FPS - screen fade]
description=Convert the fullscreen fade node's phase durations from seconds at 30Hz to seconds at 60Hz, so a fade to white or black lasts the real time it was authored for and still covers what it is there to cover.
patch=1,EE,00172744,word,3C014270 // lui $at, 0x4270      30.0 -> 60.0, seconds to frames
patch=1,EE,001728C8,word,28430168 // slti $v1, $v0, 0x168 hold cap 180 -> 360 frames

// The Rush Struggle, found 2026-09-10. Two rush attacks collide, both fighters
// enter state 250 (FUN_001F47C8) and rotate their sticks; the game counts hits
// into fighter+0xE50 and at 001D945C simply compares the two counts to pick a
// winner. Every part of that is authored in ticks: the struggle is exactly 88
// ticks in both arms, and hits land on fixed tick cadences plus one every-tick
// path taken while the fighter's rotation bit is up.
// The player's hands do not speed up with the tick rate, but the AI's do. The
// CPU has no stick - FUN_001D4370 branches on fighter+0x1278 and feeds an
// AI-driven fighter synthetic input from +0x127C/+0x1280/+0x1284 instead of the
// pad - and it steps that synthetic stick through cardinal directions once per
// tick, so at 60fps it rotates twice as fast in real time. Measured with both
// sticks turned at a true 5 rotations a second: 30fps ends 66-59 to the player,
// 60fps ends 53-47 to the CPU. The outcome flips.
// This gates ONLY the AI's rotation, on the state's own tick counter, using the
// game's own human/AI flag. The player's input is untouched.
[60FPS - rush struggle]
description=Honour the CPU's synthetic stick rotation every other tick in a Rush Struggle, so the AI cannot out-rotate the player twice as fast at 60fps as it does at 30.
patch=1,EE,000F1500,word,8C831278 // lw $v1, 0x1278($a0)    AI-driven fighter?
patch=1,EE,000F1504,word,10600005 // beqz $v1, 0x000F151C   human: unchanged
patch=1,EE,000F1508,word,00000000 // nop
patch=1,EE,000F150C,word,8C830964 // lw $v1, 0x964($a0)     the state's tick counter
patch=1,EE,000F1510,word,30630001 // andi $v1, $v1, 1
patch=1,EE,000F1514,word,14600003 // bnez $v1, 0x000F1524   odd tick: not rotating
patch=1,EE,000F1518,word,00000000 // nop
patch=1,EE,000F151C,word,080753CC // j 0x1d4f30             even tick: the real query
patch=1,EE,000F1520,word,00000000 // nop
patch=1,EE,000F1524,word,03E00008 // jr $ra
patch=1,EE,000F1528,word,0000102D // move $v0, $zero        (delay slot)
patch=1,EE,001F4938,word,0C03C540 // jal QueryCondition -> jal 000F1500

// The Beam Struggle, found 2026-09-12. Two beams collide, both fighters enter
// state 304 (FUN_001FB660) and rotate their sticks. Rotations are counted into
// fighter+0xE4C - seeded on entry from the move's power and the character's
// attributes, so it does not start at zero - and the contest itself is run by
// an event manager, FUN_001D8E50, dispatched once per tick from FUN_001D9900
// for modes 1..5. That manager is authored entirely in ticks: 30 ticks of one
// fighter's introduction, 30 of the other's, then 46 ticks in which a tug at
// [m+8] moves one step per tick toward whoever leads on +0xE4C, 16 ticks to
// show the result and a few more to drive the tug out to 71, where the sign of
// the tug names the winner and sets the outcome flags 0xC1/0xC3 and 0xC2.
// So at 60fps the whole clash plays in half its real time - 2.17s against
// 4.34s - while a human's hands do not speed up, and the CPU's do: it steps a
// synthetic stick through cardinal directions once per tick. Measured from the
// user's own save state with both sticks turned at a true 5 rotations a second,
// 30fps ends 91-88 to the player and 60fps ends 61-62 to the CPU. The outcome
// flips.
// This halves the manager's clock by doubling every phase length, which puts
// each phase back on its 30fps real-time duration. The tug still moves one step
// per tick, so over a window of twice as many ticks it reaches twice the
// magnitude - which is why the clash point constant halves too, leaving the
// point exactly where 30fps puts it while updating smoothly every frame rather
// than in 30Hz steps. The AI's rotation is gated to every other tick using the
// game's own human/AI flag, as the Rush Struggle does; the player's input is
// untouched. Against the 30fps game with the 60fps base patch alone, where the
// AI draws the same random stream, this reproduces it exactly: 72-72 idle and
// 91-86 at 5 rotations a second, over the same 4.3 seconds.
[60FPS - beam clash]
description=Pace the Beam Struggle in real time at 60fps - every phase of its event manager, the clash point, and the CPU's stick rotation - so the clash lasts as long as it does at 30fps and is decided the same way.
patch=1,EE,000F1540,word,8C831278 // lw $v1, 0x1278($a0)    AI-driven fighter?
patch=1,EE,000F1544,word,10600005 // beqz $v1, 0x000F155C   human: unchanged
patch=1,EE,000F1548,word,00000000 // nop
patch=1,EE,000F154C,word,8C830964 // lw $v1, 0x964($a0)     the state's tick counter
patch=1,EE,000F1550,word,30630001 // andi $v1, $v1, 1
patch=1,EE,000F1554,word,14600003 // bnez $v1, 0x000F1564   odd tick: not rotating
patch=1,EE,000F1558,word,00000000 // nop
patch=1,EE,000F155C,word,080753CC // j 0x1d4f30             even tick: the real query
patch=1,EE,000F1560,word,00000000 // nop
patch=1,EE,000F1564,word,03E00008 // jr $ra
patch=1,EE,000F1568,word,0000102D // move $v0, $zero        (delay slot)
patch=1,EE,001FB9BC,word,0C03C550 // jal QueryCondition -> jal 000F1540
patch=1,EE,001FB9A8,word,28420020 // slti $v0, $v0, 0x20    rotations count from tick 32
patch=1,EE,001D8EF8,word,2402003C // addiu $v0, $zero, 0x3c the second cue at tick 60
patch=1,EE,001D8F0C,word,2402003C // addiu $v0, $zero, 0x3c
patch=1,EE,001D8F14,word,2862003C // slti $v0, $v1, 0x3c    first introduction: 60 ticks
patch=1,EE,001D8F28,word,2862003C // slti $v0, $v1, 0x3c
patch=1,EE,001D8F30,word,28620078 // slti $v0, $v1, 0x78    second introduction ends at 120
patch=1,EE,001D8FD0,word,284300D4 // slti $v1, $v0, 0xd4    the tug phase runs to 212
patch=1,EE,001D9070,word,28430020 // slti $v1, $v0, 0x20    result shown for 32 ticks
patch=1,EE,001D90BC,word,2842008E // slti $v0, $v0, 0x8e    tug driven out to 142
patch=1,EE,002FD330,word,3CCCCCCC // 0.05 -> 0.025          clash point per tug step

// Solar Flare, found 2026-09-17 for issue #32. SuperCombo says it "causes
// opponent to lock off" and gives no duration; the 30fps game is the oracle.
//
// fighter+0x0FF8 is the victim's blind timer, armed at 001C82F4 from move data.
// The fighter's per-tick update counts it down and, while it is positive, sets
// flags 0x93, 0x137 and 0x96 - the lock-off:
//
//   001E1F3C  addiu $v0, $v0, -1           once a tick, after a <= 0 test
//   001E1F44  sw    $v0, 0xFF8($s1)
//   001E1F74  jal   FUN_001DC030           flash (+0xFFC) moves toward 2 * timer,
//   001E1F78  li    $a2, 15                  by at most 15 a tick
//
// The decrement is hooked on the addiu itself, not on the load: when the
// victim is not paralysed the load happens in a delay slot at 001E1EB4 and
// jumps past 001E1F30. The flash blend is given a step of 0 on odd ticks, so it
// rises at 15 per 30Hz tick. FUN_001DC030 has no other caller.
//
// Save state 3, Krillin 66 units from a standing Ultimate Gohan, L2 + Circle,
// from the pnach:
//
//                       timer runs   flash peaks      flash rise per 2 vsyncs
//   30fps               150 vsyncs   132 at +18       15
//   60fps, shipped      75 vsyncs    132 at +9        30
//   60fps, this group   150 vsyncs   132 at +18       15
//
// The stagger itself, state 198, is 50 vsyncs in every arm.
[60FPS - solar flare]
description=Keep Solar Flare's lock-off and white flash for their real time, so a 2.5-second blind lasts 2.5 seconds at 60fps instead of 1.25.
patch=1,EE,000F1780,word,3C080033 // lui $t0, 0x0033
patch=1,EE,000F1784,word,8D081D64 // lw $t0, 0x1D64($t0)      frame counter
patch=1,EE,000F1788,word,31080001 // andi $t0, $t0, 1
patch=1,EE,000F178C,word,2442FFFF // addiu $v0, $v0, -1       the original decrement
patch=1,EE,000F1790,word,03E00008 // jr $ra
patch=1,EE,000F1794,word,00481021 // addu $v0, $v0, $t0       odd tick: undo it
patch=1,EE,000F17A0,word,3C080033 // lui $t0, 0x0033
patch=1,EE,000F17A4,word,8D081D64 // lw $t0, 0x1D64($t0)      frame counter
patch=1,EE,000F17A8,word,31080001 // andi $t0, $t0, 1
patch=1,EE,000F17AC,word,0008300B // movn $a2, $zero, $t0     odd tick: step the flash by 0
patch=1,EE,000F17B0,word,0807700C // j 0x001DC030             the blend, returning to its caller
patch=1,EE,000F17B4,word,00000000 // nop
patch=1,EE,001E1F3C,word,0C03C5E0 // addiu $v0, -1 -> jal 000F1780   blind ticks left, after the <= 0 test
patch=1,EE,001E1F74,word,0C03C5E8 // jal 001DC030 -> jal 000F17A0
